nL_2 &: y = 1 \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, parallel to the }x\\text{-axis, thus not sunny)},\\\\[2pt]\nL_3 &: y = x \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, not }0,\\infty,\\text{or }-1,\\text{ hence sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe now verify that each required point lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Containing line(s) | Check |\n|-----------------|--------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_1\\), \\(L_2\\), \\(L_3\\) | \\(x=1\\) ✓, \\(y=1\\) ✓, \\(y=x\\) gives \\(1=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(x=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(x=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(y=1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(y=x\\) gives \\(2=2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(y=1\\) ✓ |\n\nAll six points are covered.\n\n**Sunny‑line count.**  \n- \\(L_1\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis → not sunny.  \n- \\(L_2\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis → not sunny.  \n- \\(L_3\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence \\(L_3\\) is sunny.\n\nThus exactly one of the three lines is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ a configuration with exactly }k=1\\text{ sunny line exists.}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision