ine? Maybe L9? L9 is y = -1.5 x + 7, for x=4 gives y = 1, not 2. So no. Could we replace L8 with line covering (4,2) and (3,2)? That's line through (3,2) and (4,2): horizontal, not sunny. So can't. Could we replace L6 with line covering (3,2) and (5,1)? Slope = (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. Equation: y - 1 = -0.5 (x - 5) => y = -0.5 x + 3.5. For x=3, y = -1.5 + 3.5 = 2, good. So line L10: y = -0.5 x + 3.5 passes through (5,1) and (3,2). It also passes through (4,1)? For x=4, y = -2 + 3.5 = 1.5, not integer. So L10 covers (3,2) and (5,1). So we can replace L6 with L10. Then we have lines:\n\n- L1: y = x (covers (2,2),(3,3)).\n- L9: y = -1.5 x + 7 (covers (2,4),(4,1)).\n- L8: y = 0.5 x (covers (2,1),(4,2)).\n- L10: y = -0.5 x + 3.5 (covers (3,2),(5,1)).\n\nNow covered: (2,1),(2,2),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Uncovered: (2,3),(3,1). (2,3) not covered. (3,1) not covered.\n\nWe could try to adjust L9 to also cover (2,3) maybe? L9 is y = -1.5 x + 7, for x=2 gives y = -3 + 7 = 4, not 3. So no. Could we adjust L1 to cover (2,3)? L1 is y = x, no. Could we replace L1 with line covering (2,3) and (3,1)? That's slope = (3-1)/(2-3) = 2/(-1) = -2, sunny. Equation: y - 1 = -2 (x - 3) => y = -2x + 7. For x=2, y = -4 + 7 = 3, good. So line L11: y = -2x + 7 covers (2,3) and (3,1). So we can replace L1 with L11. Then we have lines:\n\n- L11: y = -2x + 7 (covers (2,3),(3,1)).\n- L9: y = -1.5 x + 7 (covers (2,4),(4,1)).\n- L8: y = 0.5 x (covers (2,1),(4,2)).\n- L10: y = -0.5 x + 3.5 (covers (3,2),(5,1)).\n\nNow covered: (2,1),(2,3),(2,4),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Uncovered: (2,2),(3,3). (2,2) and (3,3) are not covered. Could we adjust L9 to also cover (2,2) maybe? L9: y = -1.5 x + 7, for x=2 gives y = 4, not 2. So no. Could we adjust L8 to cover (2,2)? L8: y = 0.5 x, for x=2 gives y=1, not 2. So no. Could we adjust L10 to cover (2,2)? L10: y = -0.5 x + 3.5, for x=2 gives y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not 2. So no. Could we replace one line with line covering (2,2) and (3,3)? T