\n- Pair (1,4) with (2,5?) not in set. So (1,4) is left. Could pair (1,4) with (3,2) maybe? Slope = (4-2)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, not sunny. Pair (1,4) with (2,1): slope = (4-1)/(1-2) = 3/(-1) = -3, sunny. So line L4: through (1,4) and (2,1) slope -3, equation: y - 1 = -3 (x - 2) => y = -3x + 7. This line also passes through (3,? ) x=3 => y = -9 + 7 = -2, not in set. So L4 covers (1,4),(2,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered (1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(2,3),(1,3),(2,4),(1,4),(2,1). Uncovered: (3,1),(3,2). That's 2 points left, but we have used all 4 sunny lines. So we need to adjust to cover (3,1) and (3,2) as well. Could we modify some lines to include them? For example, line L1 (y = x) could also include (3,3) but that's diagonal. Not needed. L2 (y = x+1) includes (3,4) not in set. L3 (y = x+2) includes (3,5) not in set. L4 (y = -3x + 7) includes (3,-2) not in set. So none cover (3,1) or (3,2). Could we replace L4 with a line covering (1,4),(2,1),(3,2) maybe? Let's see if (1,4),(2,1),(3,2) are collinear. Compute slopes: (1,4)-(2,1): slope = (4-1)/(1-2) = 3/(-1) = -3. (2,1)-(3,2): slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/(-1) = 1. So not collinear. So can't.\n\nCould we replace L3 with line covering (1,3),(2,4),(3,5?) not in set. So no.\n\nCould we replace L2 with line covering (1,2),(2,3),(3,4?) not in set.\n\nThus we need to incorporate (3,1) and (3,2) into some lines. Perhaps we can pair (3,1) with (1,2) maybe? Slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. So line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope -0.5, equation: y = -0.5 x + 2.5. This line also passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So we could replace L2 (which covered (1,2),(2,3)) with a line covering (1,2),(3,1). But then (2,3) becomes uncovered. Could we cover (2,3) with another line? Maybe pair (2,3) with (4,1) but (4,1) is diagonal. Pair (2,3) with (3,2): slope = (3-2)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny. Pair (2,3) with (1,4): slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not sunny. Pair (2,3) with (5,1) not in set. So (2,3) is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, we could pair (3,