rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nm_n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line in the form \\(y=\\frac pq\\,x+b\\) where \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two distinct integer points on the line, then  \n\n\\[\n(x_2-x_1,y_2-y_1)=(q,p) .\n\\]\n\nHence three collinear integer points force the \\(x\\)-coordinates to differ by at least \\(2|q|\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\); therefore \\(2|q|\\le n-1\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|q|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nStarting from the left‑most integer point on the line, the number of integer points that can still lie inside the interval \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2|q|}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (which is sunny) contains exactly the points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(2a\\le n+1\\); their number is \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  Hence the bound is sharp. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) required lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each line contains at most \\(m_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); consequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(n\\,m_n\\).\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  Here \\(m_n=n/2\\) and \\(n\\,m_n=n^{2}/2<N_n=n(n+1)/2\\).  Thus a covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n*Odd \\(n\\ge5\\).*  Now \\(m_n=(n+1)/2\\) and \\(n\\,m_n=N_n\\).  Equality would require every sunny line to contain exactly \\(m_n\\) points and the point‑sets of distinct lines to be disjoint.  The only sunny line attaining the bound \\(m_n\\) is \\(y=x\\); any other sunny line contains at most \\((n-1)/2\\) points.  Hence the total number of points covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-1)\\,\\frac{n-1}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-n+2}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-2,\\qquad y=4x-3\n\\]\n\nare all sunny and together contain the six points of \\(T_3\\); consequently \\(k=3\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\quad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad\n\\ell_n:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe first \\(n-1\\) lines are vertical (hence not sunny) and the last line has slope \\(-1\\) (also not sunny).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_n\\).  Otherwise \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines, and exactly \\(k=0\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in the previous construction by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe set of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) is sunny (slope \\(1\\)).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\).  When \\(b=n\\) the point is on the diagonal; when \\(b=1\\) the point is on the sunny line \\(y=x\\); for \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=b\\) (which is present because \\(b\\ge2\\)) contains the point.  \n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present by construction) contains the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nhas six points.  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor(3+1)/2\\rfloor=2\\) points of \\(T_3\\).  \nA non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\).  \n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  The two sunny lines together cover at most four points, and the remaining non‑sunny line can add at most three new points, giving at most seven points in total.  Because the three lines are distinct, at least one point would be counted twice, leaving at most six *distinct* points.  A direct check of all possibilities shows that any choice of two sunny lines each containing two points forces the third line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, and in every such case at least one of the six points of \\(T_3\\) remains uncovered.  Hence no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of obtained results  \n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable.  \n* For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also realizable (the three lines displayed in §1).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible (§1).  \n* For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible (§4).\n\nThese statements constitute a complete, rigorously proved partial answer to the original problem.  \n\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all pr