ine covering (3,2) and (4,1)? The slope would be (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So any line covering any two of these three points is non-sunny. So we cannot cover them with two sunny lines. Could we use a non-sunny line for one of them? But we already used two non-sunny lines (vertical x=1 and x=2). We could use a third non-sunny line, but we only have 4 lines total, and we want exactly 2 sunny lines. So we have 2 non-sunny lines left. Actually we have 2 non-sunny lines (vertical x=1 and x=2) and we need 2 sunny lines. So we cannot use a third non-sunny line. So we cannot cover the remaining three points with two sunny lines. So that fails.\n\nWhat about using a non-sunny line of slope -1? Could we cover some points? The line x+y=c. For points (3,1) and (2,3): 3+1=4, 2+3=5, not same. (3,2) and (2,3): 5 and 5, yes. So line x+y=5 passes through (3,2) and (2,3). But (2,3) is already covered by vertical x=2. So we could use line x+y=5 to cover (3,2) and (2,3). But (2,3) is already covered, but that's okay. Then we still need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). Could we use a sunny line to cover (3,1) and (4,1)? That's horizontal, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we use a sunny line to cover (3,1) and (2,2)? (2,2) is covered by vertical x=2. But line through (3,1) and (2,2) has slope (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we use a sunny line to cover (4,1) and (2,2)? Slope (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, which is sunny. So line through (4,1) and (2,2) is sunny. That line also passes through (3,1.5) which is not integer. So we could have a sunny line covering (4,1) and (2,2). Then we need to cover (3,1). Could we use a sunny line covering (3,1) and some other point already covered? For example (3,1) and (1,3) slope (3-1)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, non-sunny. (3,1) and (1,2) slope (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. So line through (3,1) and (1,2) is sunny. So we could have two sunny lines: L1 through (4,1) and (2,2) (slope -0.5), L2 through (3,1) and (1,2) (slope -0.5 as well? Actually slope (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, same slope as L1, but they are distinct lines because they pass through different points. So both are sunny. So we have vertical x=1, vertical x=2, sunny line L1, sunny line L2. Does this cover all points? Let's list points:\n\n- (1,1): covered by vertical x=1.\n- (1,2): covered by L2 (since L2 passes through (1,2) and (3,1)).\n- (1,3): covered by vertical x=1? Actually vertical x=1 covers all points with x=1, so (1,3) covered.\n- (1,4): covered by vertical x=1.\n- (2,1): covered by vertical x=2.\n- (2,2): covered by L1 (since L1 passes through (2,2) and (4,1)).\n- (2,3): covered by vertical x=2.\n- (3,1): covered by L2.\n- (3,2): not covered yet. Could be covered by vertical x=3? Not present. Could be covered by L1? L1 passes through (2,2) and (4,1). Does it pass through (3,1.5)? No. So (3,2) is uncovered. Could be covered by L2? L2 passes through (1,2) and (3,1). Does it pass through (3,2)? No. So (3,2) is uncovered. Could be covered by a non-sunny line? We have no more lines. So this fails.\n\nThus that attempt fails.\n\nMaybe we could use a non-sunny line of slope -1 to cover (3,2) and (2,3). That would be x+y=5. Then we could have vertical x=1, vertical x=2, sunny line L1 covering (4,1) and (2,2), and sunny line L2 covering (3,1) and (1,2). That would be 4 lines: x=1, x=2, x+y=5, L1, L2? Actually that's 5 lines. We need only 4 lines. So not possible.\n\nThus seems plausible that k=2 is impossible for n=4.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: Could there be a configuration with 2 sunny lines and 2 non-sunny lines that are not vertical? For example, one non-sunny line could be horizontal y=1, covering many points: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Another non-sunny line could be diagonal x+y=5 covering (2,3),(3,2). Then we have two sunny lines covering the rest. Let's try: Use y=1 (non-sunny) covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Use x+y=5 (non-sunny) covers (2,3),(3,2). Then we need to cover remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2). That's 4 points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's see: Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (2,2)? Slope (2-2)/(2-1)=0, horizontal, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (1,3)? That's vertical, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (1,4)? vertical, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (2,3)? (2,3) is already covered by x+y=5, but line through (1,2) and (2,3) slope (3-2)/(2-1)=1, sunny. So we could have a sunny line L1: y = x+1 (through (1,2),(2,3)). That covers (1,2) and (2,3) (but (2,3) already covered). Then we need to cover (1,3),(1,4),(2,2). Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,2)? Slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,2) is not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,4)? (2,4) not in T_4 (since 2+4=6 >5). So not possible. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (3,1)? (3,1) is covered by y=1, but line slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we have a sunny line covering (1,4) and (2,2)? slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2, which is sunny. So we could have L2: line through (1,4) and (2,2). That covers (1,4) and (2,2). Then we still need to cover (1,3). Could we have a sunny line covering (1,3) and (2,2)? slope -1, not allowed. (1,3) and (2,4) not in set. (1,3) and (3,1) slope -1, not allowed. (1,3) and (4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, which is sunny. So line through (1,3) and (4,1) slope -2/3, which is sunny. But we already used two sunny lines: L1 (y=x+1) and L2 (through (1,4),(2,2)). We cannot add a third sunny line. So we need to cover (1,3) with one of those two sunny lines. L1 passes through (1,2) and (2,3). Does it also pass through (1,3)? No. L2 passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Does it pass through (1,3)? No. So (1,3) remains uncovered. So that fails.\n\nThus seems plausible that k=2 is impossible for n=4.\n\nBut we need to be sure that the solution's proof is correct for all n≥4. The proof uses lemmas and seems plausible. However, we need to check if there is any subtle flaw. Let's examine the lemmas more carefully.\n\nLemma 1: For a fixed column x=a, a vertical line x=c meets the column in all points if c=a, else none. A horizontal line y=c meets the column in at most one point (if (a,c) ∈ T_n). A line of slope -1 (x+y=c) meets the column in at most one point (if (a,c-a) ∈ T_n). So any non-sunny line that is not vertical contributes at most one point to a given column. This is correct.\n\nLemma 2: For a ≤ n-2, column x=a contains at least three points: (a,1),(a,2),(a,3) because a+3 ≤ (n-2)+3 = n+1. So correct.\n\nLemma 3: They assume a family of n distinct lines covering T_n contains at most n-2 non-sunny lines. Actually they assume at most n-2 non-sunny lines (i.e., at least 2 sunny lines). They let v be number of vertical lines among those non-sunny lines. If a column x=a (a ≤ n-2) does not contain a vertical line, then each point of that column must be supplied by a distinct non-vertical non-sunny line (by Lemma 1). Since column has at least three points, we need at least three non-vertical non-sunny lines for that column. So (n-2)-v ≥ 3, i.e., v ≤ n-5. So if any column lacks a vertical line, v ≤ n-5. So there are at most n-5 vertical lines. So there are at least (n-2) - (n-5) = 3 columns lacking vertical lines. Actually they say \"If even a single one of them lacked a vertical line, we would already need three extra non‑vertical lines for that column, and the same argument applied to any other such column would quickly exceed the budget of n-2 non‑sunny lines.\" This is a bit vague but they intend to argue that you cannot have any column lacking a vertical line because you would need too many non-vertical lines. But we need to check if it's indeed impossible to have exactly three columns lacking vertical lines and exactly three non-vertical lines covering them. Let's examine that scenario.\n\nSuppose we have n=5 (so n-2=3 non-sunny lines). Suppose we have v vertical lines among them. If v ≤ n-5 = 0, then v=0. So all three non-sunny lines are non-vertical. Then we have no vertical lines. Then we have columns x=1,2,3 (since n-2=3). Each column has at least three points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have three non-vertical lines, each can intersect each column at most one point. So total points covered by non-vertical lines across all columns is at most 3*3 = 9 points (since each line can intersect each column at most once, but each line can intersect each column at most one point, but across columns each line can intersect each column at one point, so each line can cover at most one point per column, i.e., up to 3 points total (one per column). So three lines can cover at most 9 points. But there are 9 points in the three columns. So it's possible that each line covers exactly one point in each column, covering all points. However, we also need to cover points in columns x=4 and x=5? Wait, for n=5, T_5 includes points with a+b ≤ 6. So columns x=4 and x=5 also have points: column x=4 has points (4,1),(4,2) because 4+2=6; column x=5 has point (5,1). So we have additional columns beyond x=n-2=3. The solution's argument about vertical lines only considered columns up to n-2. The columns beyond n-2 are x=n-1 and x=n. Those have fewer points: column x=n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) (if n-1+2 ≤ n+1 => n+1 ≤ n+1, yes). Column x=n has point (n,1). So those three points are the \"uncovered points\" after vertical lines in columns 1..n-2. So if we have no vertical lines, we need to cover all points in columns 1..n-2 plus the three points in columns n-1 and n. So total points = sum_{a=1}^{n-2} (n+1-a) + 3 = (n-2)(n+1) - (n-2)(n-1)/2 + 3? Actually sum_{a=1}^{n-2} (n+1-a) = sum_{k=3}^{n+?} Let's compute: For a from 1 to n-2, number of points in column a = n+1-a. So sum = (n+1-1)+(n+1-2)+...+(n+1-(n-2)) = n + (n-1) + ... + 3 = sum_{i=3}^{n} i = (n(n+1)/2 - 3)?? Let's compute: sum_{i=1}^{n} i = n(n+1)/2. Subtract sum_{i=1}^{2} i = 3. So sum_{i=3}^{n} i = n(n+1)/2 - 3. So total points in columns 1..n-2 = n(n+1)/2 - 3. Then plus the three points in columns n-1 and n gives total = n(n+1)/2, which matches total points. So indeed covering all points.\n\nNow, if we have no vertical lines, we have n-2 non-sunny lines (all non-vertical). For n=5, that's 3 non-sunny lines. They can each intersect each column at most once. So across columns 1..3, each line can cover at most 3 points (one per column). So total coverage across those columns is at most 9 points. There are exactly 9 points in those columns (since sum_{i=3}^{5} i = 3+4+5 = 12? Wait, for n=5, columns 1..3 have points: column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) because 1+4=5 ≤6? Actually a+b ≤ n+1 = 6. So column 1 has points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5)? Let's compute: b ≤ n+1 - a = 6-1 =5, so b can be 1..5. So column 1 has 5 points. Column 2: b ≤ 6-2 =4, so points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Column 3: b ≤ 6-3 =3, points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). So columns 1..3 have 5+4+3 = 12 points. Wait, earlier we thought each column has at least 3 points, but for small a they have more. So the number of points in columns 1..n-2 is larger than 3*(n-2). Indeed, column a has n+1-a points. So total points in columns 1..n-2 = sum_{a=1}^{n-2} (n+1-a) = sum_{i=3}^{n} i = (n(n+1)/2 - 3). For n=5, that's (5*6/2 - 3) = (15 - 3) = 12. Yes, matches 12 points. So there are 12 points in columns 1..3. And we have 3 non-vertical lines. Each line can intersect each column at most one point, so each line can cover at most (n-2) points across those columns (one per column). So total coverage across columns 1..n-2 is at most (n-2)*(n-2) = (n-2)^2 points. For n=5, that's 3^2 = 9 points. But there are 12 points to cover. So it's impossible to cover all points in columns 1..n-2 with only non-vertical lines. So we need vertical lines. Indeed, the argument that you need vertical lines in each column is necessary because the number of points in those columns is larger than (n-2)*(n-2). Let's compute: total points in columns 1..n-2 = sum_{i=3}^{n} i = n(n+1)/2 - 3. The maximum points covered by (n-2) non-vertical lines across those columns is (n-2)*(n-2) = (n-2)^2. Compare: n(n+1)/2 - 3 vs (n-2)^2. For n≥4, which is larger? Let's compute difference: n(n+1)/2 - 3 - (n-2)^2 = (n^2 + n)/2 - 3 - (n^2 - 4n + 4) = (n^2 + n)/2 - 3 - n^2 + 4n - 4 = -(n^2)/2 + (n)/2 + 4n - 7 = -(n^2)/2 + (9n)/2 - 7 = ( -n^2 + 9n - 14 )/2 = ( -(n^2 - 9n + 14) )/2 = ( -( (n-7)(n-2) ) )/2? Let's factor n^2 - 9n + 14 = (n-7)(n-2). Indeed, (n-7)(n-2) = n^2 - 9n + 14. So difference = -( (n-7)(n-2) )/2 = ( (7-n)(n-2) )/2. For n≥7, (7-n) ≤ 0, so difference ≤ 0, meaning (n-2)^2 ≥ total points? Let's check: For n=7, difference = (7-7)*(5)/2 = 0. So for n=7, total points in columns 1..5 = (7*8/2 - 3) = 28 - 3 = 25. (n-2)^2 = 5^2 = 25. So they are equal. For n>7, (7-n) negative, difference negative, meaning (n-2)^2 > total points. So for n≥8, the number of points in columns 1..n-2 is less than (n-2)^2, so it's possible that non-vertical lines could cover all points in those columns. However, we also need to cover the three points in columns n-1 and n. So we need to consider those as well.\n\nThus the argument that you need vertical lines in each column for all n≥4 may not hold for n≥8. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe solution's Lemma 3 says: \"If a column x=a with a ≤ n-2 does not contain a vertical line, then each point of that column must be supplied by a distinct non‑vertical non‑sunny line. Lemma 2 tells us that such a column has at least three points, so we would need at least three non‑vertical non‑sunny lines for that column.\" This is correct. Then they say: \"Since the total number of non‑vertical non‑sunny lines equals (n-2)-v, the existence of any column without a vertical line forces v ≤ n-5.\" That's correct.\n\nThen they say: \"But there are exactly n-2 columns with indices 1,2,…,n-2. If even a single one of them lacked a vertical line, we would already need three extra non‑vertical lines for that column, and the same argument applied to any other such column would quickly exceed the budget of n-2 non‑sunny lines. The only way to stay within the budget is to place a vertical line in every column x=1,…,x=n-2; therefore v=n-2, and the remaining two lines (if any) must be sunny, otherwise we would have more than n-2 non‑sunny lines.\"\n\nThe key step: \"If even a single one of them lacked a vertical line, we would already need three extra non‑vertical lines for that column, and the same argument applied to any other such column would quickly exceed the budget of n-2 non‑sunny lines.\" This is not rigorous. It suggests that having one column lacking a vertical line forces at least three non-vertical lines, but you could have exactly three non-vertical lines and the rest vertical lines. That would give v = (n-2) - 3 vertical lines. So you would have v = n-5 vertical lines, and three non-vertical lines. That would be within the budget of n-2 non-sunny lines. So the argument that you cannot have any column lacking a vertical line is not fully justified. However, they then argue that the only way to stay within the budget is to place a vertical line in every column. This is a stronger claim that may be false for some n.\n\nWe need to examine if there exists a covering with exactly n-2 non-sunny lines, where some of them are non-vertical, and the rest vertical, and exactly two sunny lines, that covers all points. The solution claims it's impossible. But we need to verify if there is a counterexample for some n≥4, maybe n≥8.\n\nLet's try to find a construction for n=8 with k=2. That would be a covering of T_8 (points with a+b ≤ 9) using 8 lines, exactly 2 sunny lines, and 6 non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/-1). The solution's argument suggests that you need vertical lines in columns 1..6 (since n-2 = 6). But maybe you could have some non-vertical non-sunny lines covering many points across columns, reducing the need for vertical lines.\n\nWe need to see if we can construct a covering with 2 sunny lines and 6 non-sunny lines for n=8. Let's try to think systematically.\n\nFirst, count points: |T_n| = n(n+1)/2 = 8*9/2 = 36 points.\n\nWe need to cover 36 points with 8 lines. Each line can cover many points. Non-sunny lines are vertical (x=c), horizontal (y=c), or slope -1 (x+y=c). Each vertical line x=c covers all points with that x coordinate, i.e., points (c,b) for b from 1 to n+1-c. So vertical line x=c covers n+1-c points. Horizontal line y=c covers points (a,c) for a from 1 to n+1-c. So also n+1-c points. Diagonal line x+y=c covers points (a,b) with a+b=c, where a,b≥1, a+b ≤ n+1, so c ranges from 2 to n+1. For each c, the line x+y=c contains points (a,c-a) for a from max(1,c-(n+1)) to min(n,c-1). So number of points on diagonal line x+y=c is something like min(c-1, n+1-c). For c near middle, it's about floor((n+1)/2). So diagonal lines can cover many points as well.\n\nSunny lines can be any other slope, but they can also cover many points if they pass through many points of T_n. However, typical slopes not aligned with grid may only pass through at most 2 points (since points are integer coordinates). But there could be lines with rational slopes that pass through many points, e.g., line y = x (slope 1) passes through points (a,a) for a from 1 to floor((n+1)/2). But slope 1 is sunny (since it's not 0, ∞, -1). So sunny lines can also cover many points. Indeed, slope 1 lines are sunny. So we could use sunny lines of slope 1 to cover many points.\n\nThus the solution's claim that at most one sunny line is possible may be false because we could use two sunny lines of slope 1 and -2, etc., to cover many points. But we need to check if we can cover all points with only two sunny lines and the rest non-sunny lines.\n\nLet's try to find a construction for n=8 with k=2. We need 8 lines total. Let's try to use vertical lines for many columns, and use two sunny lines to cover the remaining points.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for columns 1..6 (i.e., x=1,...,x=6). That's 6 vertical lines (non-sunny). Then we have two remaining columns: x=7 and x=8. Points in column x=7: (7,1),(7,2). Points in column x=8: (8,1). So we have three points left: (7,1),(7,2),(8,1). As in the solution's Lemma 4. They claim a sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. But is that true? Let's check slopes between these points:\n\n- (7,1) and (7,2): vertical, non-sunny.\n- (7,1) and (8,1): horizontal, non-sunny.\n- (7,2) and (8,1): slope (1-2)/(8-7) = -1, non-sunny.\n\nThus any line through any two of these three points is non-sunny. So a sunny line can cover at most one of them. So with two sunny lines, we can cover at most two of these three points, leaving one uncovered. So we need at least three sunny lines to cover all three points if we only have vertical lines for columns 1..6. But we have only two sunny lines. So we need to adjust the construction: maybe we can use some non-vertical non-sunny lines (horizontal or diagonal) to cover some of those points, reducing the need for sunny lines to cover them. But we have only two sunny lines, but we can have other non-sunny lines besides vertical lines. The total number of non-sunny lines is n - k = 8 - 2 = 6. So we have 6 non-sunny lines. In the above plan, we used 6 vertical lines, leaving no room for any horizontal or diagonal lines. But we could replace some vertical lines with other non-sunny lines to cover points more efficiently, perhaps covering the points (7,1),(7,2),(8,1) with non-sunny lines, leaving the sunny lines to cover other points.\n\nBut the solution's argument suggests that any column x ≤ n-2 (i.e., x ≤ 6) must have a vertical line, because otherwise you need too many non-vertical lines. However, maybe we can use diagonal lines to cover points in those columns, reducing the need for vertical lines. Let's examine.\n\nWe have 6 non-sunny lines to allocate. Suppose we use vertical lines for columns 1..5 (i.e., x=1,...,x=5). That's 5 vertical lines. Then we have one remaining non-sunny line, which could be a diagonal or horizontal line. Then we have two sunny lines. So total lines = 5 vertical + 1 non-sunny (non-vertical) + 2 sunny = 8. Let's see if we can cover all points.\n\nPoints in columns 1..5 are covered by vertical lines. That covers all points with x=1..5. The remaining points are those with x=6,7,8. Let's list them:\n\n- Column x=6: points (6,1),(6,2),(6,3) because 6+3=9 ≤9. So (6,1),(6,2),(6,3).\n- Column x=7: (7,1),(7,2).\n- Column x=8: (8,1).\n\nTotal remaining points: (6,1),(6,2),(6,3),(7,1),(7,2),(8,1) = 6 points.\n\nWe have 1 non-sunny line (non-vertical) and 2 sunny lines to cover these 6 points. Let's see if we can do that.\n\nWe could use a diagonal line x+y=c to cover some of these points. For example, line x+y=9 passes through (6,3),(7,2),(8,1). That's three points! And slope -1, which is non-sunny. So we can use the one non-sunny line as diagonal x+y=9, covering (6,3),(7,2),(8,1). Then we have remaining points: (6,1),(6,2),(7,1). That's three points left. We have two sunny lines to cover them. Can we cover these three points with two sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nPoints: (6,1),(6,2) are vertical line x=6 (non-sunny). So we cannot use a sunny line to cover both. But we could use a sunny line to cover (6,1) and (7,1) maybe? That's horizontal line y=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. (6,2) and (7,1) slope (1-2)/(7-6) = -1, non-sunny. So any line covering any two of these three points is non-sunny. So we need three sunny lines to cover them individually, but we only have two. So this fails.\n\nAlternatively, we could use the non-sunny line as a horizontal line y=1, covering (6,1),(7,1),(8,1). That's three points. Then we have remaining points: (6,2),(6,3),(7,2). That's three points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's see: (6,2) and (7,2) are horizontal line y=2, non-sunny. (6,2) and (6,3) vertical line x=6, non-sunny. (6,3) and (7,2) slope (2-3)/(7-6) = -1, non-sunny. So any line covering any two of these three points is non-sunny. So again need three sunny lines.\n\nThus using 5 vertical lines and one non-vertical non-sunny line seems insufficient.\n\nWhat if we use 4 vertical lines (x=1..4) and 2 non-vertical non-sunny lines (maybe diagonal lines) and 2 sunny lines. That gives total non-sunny lines = 4+2 = 6, sunny = 2. Let's try to cover columns 5..8 with the non-vertical lines and sunny lines.\n\nPoints in columns 5..8:\n\n- Column 5: points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3),(5,4) because 5+4=9. So (5,1)-(5,4).\n- Column 6: (6,1),(6,2),(6,3).\n- Column 7: (7,1),(7,2).\n- Column 8: (8,1).\n\nTotal points: 4+3+2+1 = 10 points.\n\nWe have 2 non-vertical non-sunny lines and 2 sunny lines to cover these 10 points. That's 4 lines total. Each line can cover at most maybe 4 points (if diagonal or slope 1). But we need to see if possible.\n\nWe could use diagonal lines x+y=c: For c=9, we have (5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1) - 4 points. For c=8, we have (5,3),(6,2),(7,1) - 3 points. For c=7, we have (5,2),(6,1) - 2 points. For c=6, we have (5,1) - 1 point. So we could use diagonal lines x+y=9 and x+y=8 as two non-sunny lines, covering (5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1) and (5,3),(6,2),(7,1). That covers 7 points. Remaining points: (5,1),(5,2),(6,1). That's three points left. We have two sunny lines to cover them. Let's see if we can cover them with two sunny lines.\n\nPoints: (5,1),(5,2) vertical line x=5 (non-sunny). (5,1) and (6,1) horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny). (5,2) and (6,1) slope (1-2)/(6-5) = -1, non-sunny. So any line covering any two of these three points is non-sunny. So we need three sunny lines. So fails.\n\nAlternatively, we could use a sunny line of slope 1 to cover (5,1) and (6,2) maybe? But (6,2) is already covered by diagonal x+y=8. But we could adjust