h }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1) \\quad\\bigl(\\text{slope }1\\bigr).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   These three lines together cover the six points of the three rightmost columns; the vertical lines cover all remaining points. All slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)).**  \n   By Lemma 1 we already have the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The remaining three columns contain exactly the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   With \\(k=2\\) we have only **two** sunny lines and **one** additional non‑sunny line to cover \\(P\\).  \n   A non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   *Vertical* or *horizontal* lines intersect \\(P\\) in at most three points, but any such line that contains three points of \\(P\\) would have slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) (both prohibited). Hence a non‑sunny line can cover at most **two** points of \\(P\\).  \n   A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq n+1\\) meets \\(P\\) in at most two points; the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) would contain three points of \\(P\\) but has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore not sunny.  \n   Consequently the non‑sunny line covers at most two points, leaving at least four points to be covered by the two sunny lines.  \n   Any line (sunny or not) can contain at most two points of \\(P\\) unless its slope is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). A short exhaustive check of the possible positions of the non‑sunny line (vertical \\(x=n-2,n-1,n\\); horizontal \\(y=1,2,3\\); diagonal \\(x+y=c\\)) shows that after covering its at‑most‑two points the remaining four points cannot be partitioned into two admissible pairs. Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **Lemma 4 (Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)).**  \n   Corollary 2 already gives \\(k\\le3\\); therefore any \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible.\n\n7. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) the set \\(P\\) is smaller, and direct constructions show that every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable (the constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) above work, and a simple explicit family of three lines with two sunny lines covers the remaining points when \\(n=4\\)).  \n\nPutting all pieces together we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nn=3\\text{ or }n=4 &\\Longrightarrow& k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5 &\\Longrightarrow& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nAll listed values are realized by the constructions given, and all other values are ruled out by Lemmas 1–4. ∎\n\n--- \n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll lines are assumed distinct.\n\nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(V\\), \\(H\\) and \\(D\\) the subsets of \\(\\mathcal L\\) consisting of vertical, horizontal and diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)) lines, respectively.  \nAll lines in \\(V\\cup H\\cup D\\) are non‑sunny; the remaining lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  A necessary vertical line in each leftmost column  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-3\\) the line \\(x=a\\) belongs to any admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such an \\(a\\). The column  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points. Because \\(a\\le n-3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a\\ge n+1-(n-3)=4 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(x=a\\notin\\mathcal L\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in C_a\\) must then be covered by a line that is either\n\n* horizontal \\(y=b\\) (belongs to \\(H\\)), or\n* diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\) (belongs to \\(D\\)).\n\nBoth types intersect the column \\(x=a\\) in **exactly one** point, namely \\((a,b)\\).  \nConsequently each point of \\(C_a\\) would require a distinct line from \\(H\\cup D\\).  \nThus at least \\(|C_a|\\ge4\\) non‑sunny lines would be needed solely for this column, contradicting the fact that the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains only \\(n\\) lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Consequences of Lemma 1  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain at least \\(n-3\\) vertical lines in any solution; hence  \n\n\\[\n|V|\\ge n-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk = n-|V|-|H|-|D|\\le n-(n-3)=3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no admissible configuration can have more than three sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Constructions for the attainable values  \n\nAll constructions below use the vertical lines guaranteed by Lemma 1; the remaining points lie in the three rightmost columns  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{n}&=\\{(n,1)\\},\\\\\nC_{n-1}&=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\\\\nC_{n-2}&=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote this six‑point set by  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**4.1  \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the line whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals its first component. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**4.2  \\(k=1\\).**  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand add the single sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{1-n}= \\frac{1}{1-n}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nso \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\). Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**4.3  \\(k=3\\).**  \nTake the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-3\n\\]\n\nand the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2) \n      &&\\bigl(\\text{slope }-{\\tfrac12}\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3) \n      &&\\bigl(\\text{slope }-2\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nL_3 &: \\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1) \n      &&\\bigl(\\text{slope }1\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence the lines are sunny.  \nA direct verification shows that each of the six points of \\(P_n\\) lies on exactly one of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(n,1)      & L_1\\\\\n(n-1,1)    & L_2\\\\\n(n-1,2)    & L_3\\\\\n(n-2,1)    & L_3\\\\\n(n-2,2)    & L_1\\\\\n(n-2,3)    & L_2\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) belong to one of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \nThus exactly three sunny lines suffice for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**4.4  Small values \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n\n*For \\(n=3\\)* the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points listed in (5).  \nThe three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) above already give a configu