th a single sunny line. So this configuration fails.\n\nThus maybe k=2 is impossible for n=5. The solution's proof is fairly detailed, but we need to check if it's correct. The solution uses Lemma 1 to guarantee vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-3. Then they argue that the remaining three lines must cover the six points P_n. They consider cases for the non-sunny line (vertical, horizontal, diagonal) and show contradictions. The reasoning seems plausible. We need to verify if any case is missed or if any claim is false.\n\nLet's examine the proof in Section 5: \"Impossibility of k=2 when n≥5\". They assume exactly two sunny lines exist, so there are three remaining lines covering P_n. Since only two are sunny, the third is non-sunny. They consider possibilities for the non-sunny line: vertical, horizontal, diagonal. They argue each leads to contradiction.\n\nWe need to check each case.\n\nCase 1: Non-sunny line vertical.\n\n- If vertical line x=n-2: it meets three points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). That's non-sunny. Then remaining points are (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2). They claim no sunny line can contain any pair among these three because possible slopes are 0,∞,-1. Let's verify: The three points are (n,1), (n-1,1), (n-1,2). Pairs:\n\n  - (n,1) and (n-1,1): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed for sunny.\n  - (n,1) and (n-1,2): slope (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1 not allowed.\n  - (n-1,1) and (n-1,2): vertical slope ∞ not allowed.\n\nThus indeed no sunny line can contain any pair among them. So we would need three sunny lines to cover three points, but we only have two sunny lines. So impossible.\n\n- If vertical line x=n-1: it contains (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Then remaining points are (n,1),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). They claim any sunny line through (n,1) can meet at most one of the three points of column n-2. Let's check: (n,1) and (n-2,1) slope = (1-1)/(n-2-n) = 0/(-2) = 0 not allowed. (n,1) and (n-2,2) slope = (2-1)/(n-2-n) = 1/(-2) = -1/2 allowed (sunny). (n,1) and (n-2,3) slope = (3-1)/(n-2-n) = 2/(-2) = -1 not allowed. So a sunny line through (n,1) can contain at most one of the three points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Indeed, it can contain (n-2,2) but not the others. So we have 4 points left after vertical line x=n-1: (n,1),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). We have two sunny lines left. Each sunny line can cover at most two points (maybe three if collinear with allowed slope). But can we cover these four points with two sunny lines? Let's see: we need to cover (n,1) and maybe (n-2,2) with one sunny line (slope -1/2). That line would be L1: through (n,1) and (n-2,2). Does it also contain any other point? Check if (n-2,1) lies on that line: slope from (n,1) to (n-2,1) is 0, not same. (n-2,3) slope from (n,1) is -1, not same. So L1 covers (n,1) and (n-2,2). Then we need to cover (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) with one sunny line. But any line through (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) is vertical, not allowed. So we cannot cover both with a sunny line. Could we instead pair (n-2,1) with (n-2,2) but they are already covered? Actually (n-2,2) is covered by L1. So we need to cover (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) with a single sunny line, impossible. So case fails.\n\n- If vertical line x=n: it contains (n,1) only. Then remaining points are (n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). That's 5 points. Two sunny lines can cover at most 4 points (if each covers 2). So impossible. So case fails.\n\nThus vertical case impossible.\n\nCase 2: Non-sunny line horizontal.\n\n- Horizontal line y=1 contains (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1). Then remaining points are (n-1,2),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). They claim no sunny line can contain two of these three points without slope 0,∞,-1. Let's check pairs:\n\n  - (n-1,2) and (n-2,2): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n  - (n-1,2) and (n-2,3): slope (3-2)/(n-2-(n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1 not allowed.\n  - (n-2,2) and (n-2,3): vertical slope ∞ not allowed.\n\nThus each sunny line can cover at most one of these three points, so we need three sunny lines to cover them, but we have only two. So impossible.\n\n- Horizontal line y=2 contains (n-1,2),(n-2,2). Then remaining points: (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n-2,3). That's 4 points. Two sunny lines can cover at most 4 points if each covers 2. But we need to see if we can pair them appropriately. Let's see possible pairings:\n\n  - (n,1) and (n-2,1): slope 0 not allowed.\n  - (n,1) and (n-2,3): slope (3-1)/(n-2-n) = 2/(-2) = -1 not allowed.\n  - (n,1) and (n-1,1): slope 0 not allowed.\n  - (n-1,1) and (n-2,1): slope 0 not allowed.\n  - (n-1,1) and (n-2,3): slope (3-1)/(n-2-(n-1)) = 2/(-1) = -2 allowed. So line through (n-1,1) and (n-2,3) is sunny. That covers those two points.\n\n  Then remaining points are (n,1) and (n-2,1). They cannot be covered by a sunny line (horizontal). So impossible.\n\n  - (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) vertical not allowed.\n\nThus cannot cover all four points with two sunny lines.\n\n- Horizontal line y=3 contains (n-2,3) only. Then remaining points are (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,2). That's 5 points, cannot be covered by two sunny lines. So impossible.\n\nThus horizontal case impossible.\n\nCase 3: Non-sunny line diagonal (slope -1). They claim any diagonal line x+y=c meets P_n in at most two points (except x+y=n+1 which meets three points but slope -1 not allowed). Indeed, P_n points are (n,1) sum n+1, (n-1,1) sum n, (n-1,2) sum n+1, (n-2,1) sum n-1, (n-2,2) sum n, (n-2,3) sum n+1. So diagonal x+y=n+1 passes through (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,3) three points, but slope -1 not allowed (non-sunny). So any diagonal line other than that meets at most two points. So after using a diagonal line covering at most two points, we have at least four points left, which need to be covered by two sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most two points (maybe more if collinear with allowed slope). But can we cover the remaining four points with two sunny lines? Possibly if each covers two points. But we need to check if the remaining points can be paired with allowed slopes. The solution says \"any sunny line can contain at most two of them and the four points cannot be partitioned into two admissible pairs\". They claim a direct verification of the six possible pairs shows each admissible pair forces the other two points to have prohibited slope. Let's verify quickly.\n\nSuppose diagonal line covers (n,1) and (n-2,2) (sum n+1 and n? Actually (n-2,2) sum n, not same diagonal). Wait diagonal line must have constant sum c. So possible diagonal lines that meet P_n in two points: we need two points with same sum. The sums are:\n\n- (n,1): sum n+1\n- (n-1,1): sum n\n- (n-1,2): sum n+1\n- (n-2,1): sum n-1\n- (n-2,2): sum n\n- (n-2,3): sum n+1\n\nThus pairs with same sum:\n\n- sum n+1: (n,1),(n-1,2); (n,1),(n-2,3); (n-1,2),(n-2,3). So diagonal x+y=n+1 passes through three points, not allowed.\n\n- sum n: (n-1,1),(n-2,2). So diagonal x+y=n passes through those two points.\n\n- sum n-1: only (n-2,1) alone.\n\nThus the only diagonal line (non-sunny) that can meet two points is x+y=n, covering (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). Then remaining points are (n,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,1),(n-2,3). That's four points. Can we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's see possible pairings:\n\n- (n,1) with (n-2,1): slope 0 not allowed.\n- (n,1) with (n-2,3): slope -1 not allowed.\n- (n,1) with (n-1,2): slope -1 not allowed.\n- (n-1,2) with (n-2,1): slope (1-2)/(n-2-(n-1)) = -1/(-1) = 1 allowed. So line L1: (n-1,2)-(n-2,1) slope 1.\n\n- (n-1,2) with (n-2,3): slope (3-2)/(n-2-(n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1 not allowed.\n\n- (n-2,1) with (n-2,3): vertical not allowed.\n\nThus we can pair (n-1,2) with (n-2,1) using sunny line slope 1. Then remaining points are (n,1) and (n-2,3). Their slope is (3-1)/(n-2-n) = 2/(-2) = -1 not allowed. So cannot cover them with a sunny line. So that fails.\n\nAlternatively, pair (n,1) with (n-2,2) but (n-2,2) is already covered by diagonal line? Actually diagonal line x+y=n covered (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). So (n-2,2) is already covered, cannot be used again. So we need to pair (n,1) with (n-2,3) not allowed