0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), so the lines are sunny.  \n     The six points in the three rightmost columns are covered as follows:\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c}\n     \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n     (n,1)   & L_1\\\\\n     (n-1,1) & L_2\\\\\n     (n-1,2) & L_3\\\\\n     (n-2,1) & L_3\\\\\n     (n-2,2) & L_1\\\\\n     (n-2,3) & L_2\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n     Every remaining point has first coordinate \\(\\le n-3\\) and therefore lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n     Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The universal bound \\(k\\le3\\) together with the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) leaves only the possibilities \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   The explicit constructions above show that each of these three values is indeed attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Consequently the complete classification is\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\u220e"

